diagnosis_text:
The dc_clos_bgp topology is fully healthy. Every data-plane and control-plane indicator examined contradicts the user's report. Connectivity from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) was tested twice (10 packets each) with zero loss and stable sub-2 ms round-trip times. leaf_router_0_0 has a system uptime of over 1 day 6 hours — it has not rebooted at any point near the reported complaint window. All five FRR routers show BGP sessions that were established once at lab start and have never dropped (Connections established: 1, dropped: 0 on every session). The FRR process list on leaf_router_0_0 confirms that watchfrr, zebra, bgpd, mgmtd, and staticd have all been running continuously since 22:36 with no daemon restarts. Scanning the FRR logs on leaf_router_0_0, spine_router_0_0, and spine_router_0_1 for keywords like "resetting", "NOTIFICATION", and FSM transitions found zero post-convergence events — only the normal initial-establishment sequence. All interfaces across all devices are UP. The routing tables are correct: leaf_router_0_0 has a valid BGP route to 10.0.1.0/24 via spine_router_0_0 (with a backup path through spine_router_0_1 losing best-path selection on router-ID only, which is expected eBGP behavior). The root cause is that no fault exists: the user's report of persistent flapping and a suspicion of leaf_router_0_0 rebooting in a loop is a false premise not supported by any live evidence. No device should be replaced or restarted.
